  "abstract": "We evaluate whether source-image decomposition diagnostics usefully assign residual-preserving stain-augmentation strength. An eight-class HMU study uses an exact-duplicate-controlled image split without verified patient mapping; a five-group HiESD study uses a quality-screened patient-grouped subset with ImageNet-pretrained ResNet18. Standard, fixed, uniformly weaker, global shuffled and adaptive augmentation share seed-specific initial weights, geometry and bounded training budgets. Across three seeds, HMU adaptive mean validation macro-F1 is 0.6836 versus 0.6890 for shuffled; HiESD adaptive is 0.7131 versus 0.7350 for shuffled and 0.7270 for standard. These completed results do not establish adaptive superiority. A within-class permutation control isolates source-specific assignment while preserving each class strength bank. The frozen reserved-patient test remains pending or active. Exact-duplicate auditing and independent metric verification improve reproducibility. Reweighting saved predictions illustrates known prevalence sensitivity rather than a novel metric. Rare-class support, coarse annotations, selected patient coverage and development reuse limit inference; clinical benefit and novelty are unconfirmed.",
